## 1. Formal Graph-Coefficient Space

After one common finite extension of the selected `101`-adic completion, all
three graph maps exist formally. For those fixed graph maps, the eta9
incidence equation is linear in its 249 coefficients. `UST.G3AH` gives a
split exact sequence

\[
0\longrightarrow K_{\mathcal O'}
 \longrightarrow {\mathcal O'}^{249}
 \longrightarrow {\mathcal O'}^{126}
 \longrightarrow0,
\]

with

\[
\operatorname{rank}K_{\mathcal O'}=123.
\]

Choose an integral lift `F_hat` of the selected G3AG residue member. Every
integral coefficient lift containing the same graphs is

\[
F_{\rm hat}+K_{\mathcal O'}.
\]

Those with exactly the same residue coefficients form

\[
\mathcal B=F_{\rm hat}+101K_{\mathcal O'},             \tag{1}
\]

a nonempty 123-dimensional p-adic open ball.

## 2. The Geometric Open

`UST.G3AC` proves that the same graph-incidence affine space contains a
nonempty joint Zariski-open locus on which:

1. the eta9 surface is smooth;
2. projection to the K3 is finite flat of degree three; and
3. the three eta9 coefficient rows have rank three.

The 33-dimensional Bertini family used there is an affine subspace of the
full rank-123 incidence solution space. Its smooth finite-flat rank-three
member proves that the corresponding open in the full solution space is
nonempty.

## 3. P-Adic Density Lemma

**Lemma.** Let `K` be an infinite nonarchimedean field. Every nonempty
Zariski-open subset of `A^n_K` meets every nonempty product of open K-balls.

**Proof.** Let `U` be nonempty and let `Z=A^n-U`. Since `Z` is proper, some
nonzero polynomial `f` vanishes on `Z`. A nonzero one-variable polynomial
cannot vanish on an open ball. Induction on the number of variables, viewing
`f` as a polynomial in the last variable, proves that a nonzero multivariate
polynomial cannot vanish on a product of open balls. Hence no open ball is
contained in `Z`, and every such ball meets `U`. QED.

Apply the lemma to the joint smooth finite-flat rank-three open and the ball
`B` in (1). Their intersection is nonempty.

## 4. Theorem

**Selected-Residue-Ball Smooth Graph-Prym Existence Theorem.** There is a
characteristic-zero eta9 coefficient member which:

- reduces to the selected G3AG special-fiber coefficient vector;
- contains the same formal `H+H+4H` graph maps;
- is smooth;
- is finite flat of degree three over the q79 K3;
- has coefficient rank three;
- carries the exact G3AC Prym divisor
  `q=2p^*H-Gamma_1-Gamma_2-Gamma_3`; and
- belongs to a free nine-member `E[3]` orbit.

**Proof.** The graph-incidence solutions form an affine 123-space, and the
fixed residue class is a p-adic open ball in it. The required geometric
conditions define a nonempty Zariski open by G3AC. The p-adic density lemma
gives a point in their intersection. Graph containment and the Prym divisor
are retained because the entire affine space is the kernel fiber of the same
three graph restrictions. The rank-three condition invokes the already
proved free `E[3]` action. QED.

